; A `.cbor` payload nested one level INSIDE another `.cbor` payload's collection, in both the
; array-element and the map-VALUE position. The inner byte-string read is emitted inside the outer
; collection's loop body, where the enclosing payload's reader is an `inner_de` overload — naming
; the OUTER reader there consumes the next outer item as the element's payload, so the member
; decodes plausible values and everything after it mis-frames. Only the decode side was ever wrong
; (the serializer writes the nested payload correctly), so the crate BUILDS clean and rejects valid
; CBOR: nothing short of an executed decode sees it, which is why the executed legs live elsewhere
; (`cbor_payload_nested_payloads` in tests/core/tests.rs decodes hand-derived RFC 8949 bytes;
; `cbor_nested_payloads` in tests/preserve-encodings/input.cddl carries the preserve round trip).
; This fixture is the all-profile snapshot/compile half.
;
; `*` is load-bearing on both containers: a fixed `[X]` mints a record struct whose
; `fn deserialize(raw..)` boundary resets the buffer name and masks the defect entirely.
;
; Kept OUT of cbor_in_bytes.cddl deliberately, on the precedent cbor_nonempty_payload.cddl
; documents: the ruby `cddl` gem (0.12.14) cannot PARSE an inline composite type2 controller
; (`bytes .cbor [* ...]` — exit 65, cddl-matrix/upstream-reports/ruby-cddl-inline-composite-control-arg.md), and a
; spec-parse failure poisons every rule in its fixture for the `ir_conformance_corpus` ruby sweep.
; Isolating the shape here keeps the ruby-judgeable fixtures judgeable; this one is
; compile/snapshot-gated only.
cbor_payload_nested = [
  elem_payloads: bytes .cbor [* bytes .cbor uint],
  value_payloads: bytes .cbor {* uint => bytes .cbor uint},
  after_payload: bytes .cbor uint,
  tail: text,
]
